WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.408
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:08.874
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:16.389
Unit eight: Genre Studies. Today you will define and classify texts precisely with defining relative clauses.

4
00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:19.365
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.533
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:25.338
Genre.

7
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.254
Genre means a category of texts sharing style, form or subject.

8
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:34.649
Science fiction is a genre that imagines possible futures.

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.705
Your turn. Say: Genre.

10
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.498
Genre.

11
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.332
Convention.

12
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:48.023
Convention means an established feature readers expect in a genre.

13
00:00:48.617 --> 00:00:51.708
A detective who solves the crime is a genre convention.

14
00:00:52.317 --> 00:00:54.679
Your turn. Say: Convention.

15
00:00:57.533 --> 00:00:58.488
Convention.

16
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.298
Hybridity.

17
00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:04.490
Hybridity means the mixing of different genres or forms.

18
00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:08.035
The novel's hybridity blends memoir and fantasy.

19
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:11.395
Your turn. Say: Hybridity.

20
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:15.165
Hybridity.

21
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:16.918
Subversion.

22
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:21.100
Subversion means the undermining of expected patterns.

23
00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:25.237
The film's subversion of the hero myth surprised audiences.

24
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.212
Your turn. Say: Subversion.

25
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:32.346
Subversion.

26
00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:34.096
Archetype.

27
00:01:35.083 --> 00:01:39.033
Archetype means a universal model of a character or situation.

28
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.882
The wise mentor is a familiar archetype.

29
00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:44.973
Your turn. Say: Archetype.

30
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:48.811
Archetype.

31
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:50.098
Trope.

32
00:01:51.083 --> 00:01:53.779
Trope means a commonly used theme or device.

33
00:01:54.383 --> 00:01:57.207
The 'chosen one' trope appears in countless fantasies.

34
00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:00.237
Your turn. Say: Trope.

35
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:03.869
Trope.

36
00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:05.792
Classification.

37
00:02:06.783 --> 00:02:10.385
Classification means the act of arranging things into categories.

38
00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:14.214
Classification by genre helps libraries and readers.

39
00:02:14.817 --> 00:02:17.632
Your turn. Say: Classification.

40
00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:21.793
Classification.

41
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:23.795
Expectation.

42
00:02:24.783 --> 00:02:28.687
Expectation means what readers anticipate based on experience.

43
00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:31.701
The twist defies every expectation.

44
00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:34.911
Your turn. Say: Expectation.

45
00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:38.977
Expectation.

46
00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:43.580
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:48.276
Visal, how would you define the film we watched? Horror or comedy?

48
00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:58.491
Neither, strictly. It's a genre that blends horror and comedy, and that hybridity is exactly what subverts our expectations.

49
00:02:59.583 --> 00:03:01.803
Give me an example of that subversion.

50
00:03:02.917 --> 00:03:11.406
The character who would normally die first, the cheerful best friend, is the one who survives and rescues everyone.

51
00:03:12.517 --> 00:03:14.911
Do you think genre labels are still useful?

52
00:03:16.017 --> 00:03:23.626
As starting points, yes. A writer who knows the conventions well is the one who can break them most effectively.

53
00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:26.430
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.283 --> 00:03:31.210
Grammar focus: Relative clauses defining categories.

55
00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:32.720
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.417 --> 00:03:36.891
A genre that blends horror and comedy subverts expectation.

57
00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:40.607
The character who would normally die first survives.

58
00:03:41.517 --> 00:03:44.654
A writer who knows the conventions can break them effectively.

59
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:55.845
Defining clauses identify which item or category we mean, so they take no commas. Use 'that' or 'which' for things, 'who' for people; in defining clauses 'that' is the more neutral choice.

60
00:03:56.583 --> 00:04:01.279
Be careful! Don't say: A genre who blends horror and comedy.

61
00:04:03.233 --> 00:04:06.568
Say: A genre that blends horror and comedy.

62
00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:08.786
Use 'that' or 'which' for things.

63
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.032
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:12.833 --> 00:04:14.206
Why Genres Matter.

65
00:04:14.506 --> 00:04:18.421
Genres function as unwritten contracts between writers and readers.

66
00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:24.660
A reader who opens a detective novel expects a crime, a set of clues and, eventually, a solution.

67
00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:31.544
Conventions that seem rigid, however, are constantly renegotiated as writers experiment with hybrid forms.

68
00:04:32.533 --> 00:04:37.621
The most memorable works are often those that honour a genre's archetypes while quietly subverting them.

69
00:04:38.633 --> 00:04:43.582
Classification, therefore, is less a set of fixed boxes than a map that keeps being redrawn.

70
00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:48.355
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:49.217 --> 00:04:51.251
Choose the correct defining clause.

72
00:04:51.551 --> 00:04:59.105
A, A writer, who knows the rules, can break them.. B, A writer who knows the rules can break them.. C, A writer which knows the rules can break them..

73
00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:06.431
The answer is B: A writer who knows the rules can break them..

74
00:05:07.583 --> 00:05:09.896
The wise old mentor is a classic blank.

75
00:05:10.196 --> 00:05:13.954
A, archetype. B, hybridity. C, subversion.

76
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.372
The answer is A: archetype.

77
00:05:20.517 --> 00:05:24.037
A story blank mixes memoir and fantasy shows hybridity.

78
00:05:24.337 --> 00:05:27.178
A, who. B, what. C, that.

79
00:05:30.733 --> 00:05:32.768
The answer is C: that.

80
00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:35.881
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:36.181 --> 00:05:42.308
A, defy expectations. B, deny expectations. C, defeat expectations.

82
00:05:45.867 --> 00:05:48.632
The answer is A: defy expectations.

83
00:05:49.783 --> 00:05:52.514
According to the reading, genres function as…

84
00:05:52.814 --> 00:05:57.780
A, fixed boxes. B, unwritten contracts. C, strict laws.

85
00:06:01.333 --> 00:06:04.134
The answer is B: unwritten contracts.

86
00:06:05.333 --> 00:06:08.392
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.608
Can you think of a story that subverts genre expectations?

88
00:06:29.417 --> 00:06:31.033
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:31.333 --> 00:07:04.504
One story that subverts genre expectations is a fairy-tale film in which the princess rescues herself instead of waiting for a prince. Viewers who expect a traditional romance are surprised when friendship, not marriage, becomes the happy ending. This subversion works because the film first follows the familiar conventions closely. Some people feel such twists have become a trope themselves. Even so, I think they show how genres can evolve to reflect new values.

90
00:07:04.804 --> 00:07:06.490
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:07.383 --> 00:07:12.474
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and relative clauses defining categories.

92
00:07:12.774 --> 00:07:18.259
Today's value is creative flexibility. Know the rules well, then bend them with purpose.

93
00:07:18.559 --> 00:07:21.049
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:21.349 --> 00:07:24.837
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

